{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3113","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3113","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3113\",\"parent_revision\":\"ccd7e1318ca843a84a58e378740729047601b591\",\"root_revision\":\"82d9cb85a73a9b7fa04be4be46dfaeb2947497ae9c6838b670aa700239e13710\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Docs\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: 2026-08-21\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nDuring #3060's implementation, `work-3060` delegated the entire `dh:implement-feature` skill invocation to a `dh:task-worker` subagent instead of running it inline itself. `implement-feature/SKILL.md` is written in first person for \\\"the orchestrator\\\" (query ready tasks, TeamCreate, dispatch task-workers). The receiving task-worker complied literally with that borrowed voice, adopting a coordinator role it was never assigned, and cascaded into TeamCreate plus further Agent dispatches (~$100 unplanned spend).\\n\\nRoot cause is textual, not permission-based. `task-worker.md`'s Identity section (\\\"you become whatever the task requires by loading the right skills... your job is to do the work \u2014 not to ask the manager how to do it\\\") provides no resistance to executing orchestrator-voiced skill content it's handed, and actively discourages the pushback that should fire on a role conflict. `implement-feature/SKILL.md`'s use of \\\"the orchestrator\\\" to mean \\\"whoever happens to be executing this skill\\\" is ambiguous against the plugin's actual, verified reality: exactly one agent per session \u2014 the interactive Claude Code session \u2014 is ever the orchestrator (confirmed by direct comparison of system prompts: the interactive session identifies itself as \\\"an interactive agent\\\"; a dispatched subagent's system prompt carries no such framing at all). Every dispatched agent, however many levels deep, and regardless of whether it itself dispatches further agents, acts on behalf of its own dispatcher \u2014 never on behalf of the human directly.\\n\\nSub-dispatch by a subagent is legitimate and valuable when explicitly assigned by its own dispatcher (a \\\"manager\\\" role, distinct from \\\"orchestrator\\\") \u2014 a subagent dispatching further subagents as planned decomposition is a recently-added platform capability, not inherently wrong. The defect is specifically role self-assignment \u2014 inferring a coordinating role from a loaded skill's own authorial voice rather than from an explicit assignment. This also predates a recent (~8 week) platform capability change (subagents gained the ability to dispatch subagents) that this workflow's prose was never revisited against, which is why the ambiguity existed unnoticed: previously nothing but the top-level session could ever have been in a position to misread \\\"the orchestrator\\\" as self-referential.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: Fix, four files, design already settled through direct conversation with the repo owner this session (root cause forensics via dedicated investigation, an advisor consult on fix approach, repo-owner corrections on role definition verified against live system-prompt evidence):\\n1. `plugins/development-harness/CONTEXT.md` \u2014 add Orchestrator/Manager/Worker vocabulary: orchestrator = the single interactive agent acting on behalf of the human, exactly one per session; manager = a subagent explicitly assigned by its dispatcher to decompose and dispatch a scoped piece of work, role stated in the delegation prompt/plan data, never self-inferred, never detected via harness introspection (a cross-harness requirement \u2014 this plugin targets Claude Code, Codex, and OpenCode); worker = a subagent executing one unit of work, never dispatches, reports STATUS: BLOCKED on an instruction to run dispatcher-voiced skill content it was not assigned to run.\\n2. New ADR in `plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/` capturing this decision (hard to reverse, non-obvious without the platform-capability timeline, a real resolved trade-off between planned decomposition and role self-assignment).\\n3. `plugins/development-harness/agents/task-worker.md` \u2014 rewrite the Identity section as a closed, positive job enumeration (read task via sam_task \u2192 profile_load \u2192 delegate to start-task \u2192 report \u2192 stop); state explicitly it never dispatches unless its own delegation prompt explicitly assigns the manager role for this task; replace \\\"not to ask the manager how to do it\\\" with explicit BLOCKED routing for a role conflict.\\n4. `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/post-planning.md` \u2014 one-line warning next to the `Skill(skill: \\\"dh:implement-feature\\\", ...)` instruction: this call runs inline in the current agent's own context; never delegate this invocation itself to a `dh:task-worker` subagent.\\n\\nCompanion, longer-horizon exploration items already filed and cross-referenced: #3099 (per-task failure policies incl. executor-loss), #3100 (executor-type declaration as SAM task schema data \u2014 the eventual formalization of the same vocabulary this item establishes in prose).\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer reading the documentation**, I want to **task-worker adopts orchestrator role from borrowed skill prose instead of its assigned worker role** so that **documentation is accurate and trustworthy**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: #3060 over-orchestration incident forensics \u2014 .tmp/scratch/reports/20260821-task-worker-over-orchestration.md; design settled in direct conversation with repo owner 2026-08-21/22\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-21\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>